For a fixed cross-section, \\(r\_{\text{start}}\\) and \\(\phi\_{\text{start}}\\) span the whole disk of possible departure points \\(S\\), not just the torus surface. We should therefore allow \\(0\leq r\_{\text{start}}\leq a\\).

Yes, we want to have that capability. But I was using the surface as the illustrative case. The vector departing at an n_D at any point on the surface should reconnect in a tidy ring. If that's the case for the surface, the same relationship should work for the rest of the tube... or rather, that's the case if the vector is *always* tangent to the circumference at its current position, for the whole path. But.... there's no reason we shouldn't allow geodesics of the torus. So, the null case, is not "pitch and yaw set to zero" ...it's a function of the tangent. Which is I think what I was groping for....

What we want is for the wander to always be inside the tube, and for any path on the surface of the tube to trace a neat ring if the vector is "straight" from its own frame of reference.&#x20;

It seems to me that there is an easier way to do this.... Let the torus be defined as the volume of rotation of a sphere of some radius whose origin lies at some distance from a central axis larger than its radius. Equivalently, it would be the volume of rotation of a sphere whose center sweeps a circle of diameter larger than its radius, say six times its radius... so the donut hole and the transectional cross section are the same circle at right-angles, with an intersection on the inner circumference of the torus, and any great circle on the sphere is also the same circle.&#x20;

Then, you turn the three axes of the sphere into vectors. The sphere's motion is then the additive vector of the three... if it's set to the tangent of the circular trace, that is, R_torus, then the sphere sweeps out a neat torus... oh, and our siphuncle comes in handy here... Maybe this isn't less complicated
